00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:43.843
the better children we're gonna have.

1027
00:43:46.381 --> 00:43:50.350
We shouldn't be limiting ourselves on what's possible,

1028
00:43:50.350 --> 00:43:52.420
we should not put limits on things,

1029
00:43:52.420 --> 00:43:53.888
anything is possible.

1030
00:43:53.888 --> 00:43:57.730
My oldest son will say, "Does that mean I could fly, dad?"

1031
00:43:57.730 --> 00:44:00.100
Well, I mean, the Wright Brothers figured it out.

1032
00:44:00.100 --> 00:44:01.520
(both laughing)
Yeah,

1033
00:44:01.520 --> 00:44:02.757
get on that problem.

1034
00:44:02.757 --> 00:44:05.910
Maybe, you know, just 'cause no one's done it yet.

1035
00:44:05.910 --> 00:44:07.363
Exactly.
Who knows.

1036
00:44:09.830 --> 00:44:12.257
The idea of living your values is one of them as well

1037
00:44:12.257 --> 00:44:14.104
and you touched on that a minute ago.

1038
00:44:14.104 --> 00:44:15.260
I think it's

1039
00:44:16.531 --> 00:44:19.860
an important exercise to be

1040
00:44:19.860 --> 00:44:22.460
journaling or connecting with

1041
00:44:22.460 --> 00:44:25.450
what your values are on a regular basis,

1042
00:44:25.450 --> 00:44:26.790
'cause our values are shifting

1043
00:44:26.790 --> 00:44:28.630
depending upon what's happening,

1044
00:44:28.630 --> 00:44:30.240
but I think a lot of people just,

1045
00:44:30.240 --> 00:44:33.180
we're not in a culture that is really

1046
00:44:33.180 --> 00:44:35.940
encouraging us to think about that, right,

1047
00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:37.247
so, I think there's a lot of people who are like,

1048
00:44:37.247 --> 00:44:39.800
"I don't know what my values are, I wanna

1049
00:44:39.800 --> 00:44:42.210
provide for my family and like, you know,

1050
00:44:42.210 --> 00:44:43.840
I don't know, watch football on the weekend,"

1051
00:44:43.840 --> 00:44:45.710
like, it doesn't go much further than that,

1052
00:44:45.710 --> 00:44:49.470
so, how does one who's never really wrestled with this

1053
00:44:49.470 --> 00:44:52.070
even begin to unpack what that might mean for them?

1054
00:44:52.070 --> 00:44:54.620
You know, I just had somebody that read the book,

1055
00:44:54.620 --> 00:44:56.950
I didn't know he had bought the book for his family,

1056
00:44:56.950 --> 00:44:58.130
his young children,

1057
00:44:58.130 --> 00:45:00.480
he said, "I really love this idea of values,

1058
00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:03.200
my family and I sat down and we made a list

1059
00:45:03.200 --> 00:45:06.330
and we were shocked at our oldest son who was like nine

1060
00:45:06.330 --> 00:45:08.100
and what his values were,"

1061
00:45:08.100 --> 00:45:10.680
and so, it's really as simple and as hard

1062
00:45:10.680 --> 00:45:12.810
as sitting down with a piece of paper,

1063
00:45:12.810 --> 00:45:16.910
giving it out to your wife or your husband and the kids,

1064
00:45:16.910 --> 00:45:18.410
and just taking a stab at it, like,

1065
00:45:18.410 --> 00:45:19.990
what are the things that are important to you?

1066
00:45:19.990 --> 00:45:22.030
What are the things that you value?

1067
00:45:22.030 --> 00:45:25.077
Do you and the family wanna be incredibly healthy,

1068
00:45:25.077 --> 00:45:27.500
do you wanna be known as the hardest working family

1069
00:45:27.500 --> 00:45:28.333
on the block?

1070
00:45:28.333 --> 00:45:29.699
What are the things

1071
00:45:29.699 --> 00:45:31.270
that are gonna move the needle?

1072
00:45:31.270 --> 00:45:35.320
If this was 1000 years ago and you had a family crest,

1073
00:45:35.320 --> 00:45:37.970
what would the saying be on that crest?

1074
00:45:37.970 --> 00:45:41.010
You know, and so, for us,

1075
00:45:41.010 --> 00:45:42.420
for me, one of the big things is like

1076
00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:45.470
I don't care win or lose guys, but we gotta be known

1077
00:45:45.470 --> 00:45:46.970
as the hardest working family on the planet,

1078
00:45:46.970 --> 00:45:49.050
like I can't have this business out there

1079
00:45:49.050 --> 00:45:50.620
where I'm telling people, like, we don't quit.

1080
00:45:50.620 --> 00:45:51.910
Yeah, but what if your kids

1081
00:45:51.910 --> 00:45:53.290
have a completely different set of values

1082
00:45:53.290 --> 00:45:55.300
and they're like, "That's good for you

1083
00:45:55.300 --> 00:45:57.340
but that ain't what, the life I'm living?"

1084
00:45:57.340 --> 00:45:59.820
Well, I don't care who you are,

1085
00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:02.310
you should be a hard worker, right.

1086
00:46:02.310 --> 00:46:03.430
I remember my son

1087
00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:07.570
was skiing, we were in the ski program at Killington

1088
00:46:07.570 --> 00:46:10.210
'cause they grew up on a farm in Vermont,

1089
00:46:10.210 --> 00:46:13.210
and I go and it's nice the parents get to go in

1090
00:46:13.210 --> 00:46:14.710
and have lunch with their children.

1091
00:46:14.710 --> 00:46:17.400
So, they go ski all morning with the ski instructors,

1092
00:46:17.400 --> 00:46:18.460
come in have lunch

1093
00:46:18.460 --> 00:46:20.920
and then they go out again for the second half of the day,

1094
00:46:20.920 --> 00:46:23.920
and the lady says to me, "Oh, your son,

1095
00:46:23.920 --> 00:46:25.990
he's already in, I said, "But it's only 11:30,

1096
00:46:25.990 --> 00:46:27.037
I thought it was,"

1097
00:46:27.037 --> 00:46:29.350
"Oh, well, he was cold, he came in early,"

1098
00:46:29.350 --> 00:46:32.890
and I was so furious right, but I had to temper myself.

1099
00:46:32.890 --> 00:46:35.330
It's tough to be your kid, man, all right,

1100
00:46:35.330 --> 00:46:37.147
have compassion for your kids.

1101
00:46:37.147 --> 00:46:38.682
And I go home and I wanna...
They wanna come and

1102
00:46:38.682 --> 00:46:40.440
live with me for a while.

1103
00:46:40.440 --> 00:46:43.930
I'm gonna expand on that for a second, so,

1104
00:46:43.930 --> 00:46:44.783
I'm like, Jack,

1105
00:46:46.130 --> 00:46:48.230
and he knows, right, something's coming,

1106
00:46:48.230 --> 00:46:50.177
he's probably six years old at the time,

1107
00:46:50.177 --> 00:46:53.840
"I went out, I was really cold," I said, "You know what?

1108
00:46:53.840 --> 00:46:56.090
This team might not be the right team for you,

1109
00:46:56.090 --> 00:46:57.639
you're on my ski team now,

1110
00:46:57.639 --> 00:46:58.791
(both laughing)

1111
00:46:58.791 --> 00:47:02.040
let's go outside, let's put our skis on,

1112
00:47:02.040 --> 00:47:04.320
we've got a half hour before lunch, come on,"

1113
00:47:04.320 --> 00:47:08.360
and he puts his skis on and I proceed to penguin walk up

1114
00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:11.157
the mountain with him and we're walking up.

1115
00:47:11.157 --> 00:47:11.990
"What are we doing?"

1116
00:47:11.990 --> 00:47:14.770
I said, "Oh, well, my ski team, we don't take chair lifts,

1117
00:47:14.770 --> 00:47:17.171
you'll never be cold again,"

1118
00:47:17.171 --> 00:47:19.620
(laughing) and we hike up the whole mountain,

1119
00:47:19.620 --> 00:47:21.730
I'm like, "Isn't this great,

1120
00:47:21.730 --> 00:47:23.700
you never have to go on that ski team again?"

1121
00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:26.940
We worked so hard, he never wanted to be on my team,

1122
00:47:26.940 --> 00:47:28.910
my ski team.
Yeah,

1123
00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:30.975
he's gonna tell that story to his therapist.

1124
00:47:30.975 --> 00:47:33.830
(laughs) For sure, my wife says that all the time,

1125
00:47:33.830 --> 00:47:36.610
but I want us

1126
00:47:36.610 --> 00:47:39.330
to be hard workers, obviously the parents have an influence

1127
00:47:39.330 --> 00:47:41.120
on what the family values are,

1128
00:47:41.120 --> 00:47:45.200
I don't think you could take a six-year-old and expect like,

1129
00:47:45.200 --> 00:47:46.033
you know what I mean?

1130
00:47:46.033 --> 00:47:48.210
You've gotta guide that a little bit,

1131
00:47:48.210 --> 00:47:50.770
they don't have a prefrontal cortex yet so,

1132
00:47:50.770 --> 00:47:54.000
it's not like they're gonna spit out perfect values

1133
00:47:55.160 --> 00:47:56.143
at that point,

1134
00:47:57.040 --> 00:47:58.290
you gotta guide it a bit.

1135
00:47:59.550 --> 00:48:01.570
I think there's a practicality challenge here too,

1136
00:48:01.570 --> 00:48:04.000
it's one thing if you've been doing this all along

1137
00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:05.830
since your kids were really little

1138
00:48:05.830 --> 00:48:07.134
and they're like, okay,

1139
00:48:07.134 --> 00:48:10.570
this is what we do and this is dad's deal,

1140
00:48:10.570 --> 00:48:14.890
but you come in hot when your kids are like 14, 15,

1141
00:48:14.890 --> 00:48:16.250
they ain't having it.

1142
00:48:16.250 --> 00:48:17.350
That's a fair point.

1143
00:48:18.420 --> 00:48:20.277
There's a book everybody should read called

1144
00:48:20.277 --> 00:48:22.200
"The One Minute Manager",

1145
00:48:22.200 --> 00:48:24.720
it was written by a guy named Ken Blanchard

1146
00:48:24.720 --> 00:48:27.930
and one of his concepts is,

1147
00:48:27.930 --> 00:48:30.763
when you manage people, which is like managing children,

1148
00:48:32.010 --> 00:48:36.340
you have to set really tight expectations in the beginning

1149
00:48:36.340 --> 00:48:39.760
at the moment that person joins the organization.

1150
00:48:39.760 --> 00:48:41.760
You can't let them run free

1151
00:48:41.760 --> 00:48:45.220
and then clamp down the vice grip seven months later,

1152
00:48:45.220 --> 00:48:47.760
does not work, right, they're gonna lose their mind,

1153
00:48:47.760 --> 00:48:50.460
same with your point, you can at 14 years old,

1154
00:48:50.460 --> 00:48:52.610
say all right, now here's what we're gonna do,

1155
00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:55.670
you've gotta start laying the tracks

1156
00:48:55.670 --> 00:48:57.930
early on and I definitely laid tracks early on

1157
00:48:57.930 --> 00:49:00.590
that we work hard as a family,

1158
00:49:00.590 --> 00:49:03.970
we chopped wood, we hiked up the mountain.

1159
00:49:03.970 --> 00:49:05.810
When Charlie was four years old, I might have told you this,

1160
00:49:05.810 --> 00:49:07.410
when Charlie was four years old,

1161
00:49:08.450 --> 00:49:12.470
he was not the most ideal before Christmas, so,

1162
00:49:12.470 --> 00:49:14.380
everybody got presents that year

1163
00:49:14.380 --> 00:49:16.740
but his presents I put on top of the mountain

1164
00:49:16.740 --> 00:49:19.290
and so, in his little feety pajamas with snowshoes.

1165
00:49:19.290 --> 00:49:20.540
(Joe laughing)
Oh, my God.

1166
00:49:20.540 --> 00:49:21.920
We had to go.
You've gotta be kidding me.

1167
00:49:21.920 --> 00:49:24.890
There was a note from Santa that said, "Look, Charlie,

1168
00:49:24.890 --> 00:49:27.360
I'm sorry but because you didn't give a 1000%

1169
00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:29.580
I couldn't get the presents to the house,

1170
00:49:29.580 --> 00:49:31.060
they're on top of the mountain."

1171
00:49:31.060 --> 00:49:32.462
Get out of here, you.
Yeah. (laughs)

1172
00:49:32.462 --> 00:49:34.870
(laughing) Oh, my God.

1173
00:49:34.870 --> 00:49:36.789
So, we, this is not new,

1174
00:49:36.789 --> 00:49:40.400
they know what they're getting into with us,

1175
00:49:40.400 --> 00:49:41.450
my wife is definitely

1176
00:49:43.260 --> 00:49:44.890
the softer side,

1177
00:49:44.890 --> 00:49:46.100
now I will tell you

1178
00:49:48.500 --> 00:49:51.890
well, you know, we put on this Death Camp on the farm, right

1179
00:49:51.890 --> 00:49:54.100
and there were many kids at the Death Camp

1180
00:49:54.100 --> 00:49:55.760
that were saying to me, "Oh, my God,

1181
00:49:55.760 --> 00:49:56.867
this is so hard," I said,

1182
00:49:56.867 --> 00:49:59.430
"Can you imagine what it's like being my kid?

1183
00:49:59.430 --> 00:50:01.710
When you guys leave and you get to go home,

1184
00:50:01.710 --> 00:50:03.950
my kids, this is their home."

1185
00:50:03.950 --> 00:50:05.920
I wanna read their book
(Joe laughs)

1186
00:50:05.920 --> 00:50:09.403
when they're 28.
Yeah.

1187
00:50:09.403 --> 00:50:11.010
But...
You gotta get

1188
00:50:11.010 --> 00:50:13.410
values straight.
Yeah, values are, you know,

1189
00:50:13.410 --> 00:50:16.860
look, I completely 1000% I'm on board with that,

1190
00:50:16.860 --> 00:50:18.390
I mean, I think

1191
00:50:18.390 --> 00:50:21.340
the tricky thing that I've experienced as a parent

1192
00:50:22.420 --> 00:50:23.810
is

1193
00:50:23.810 --> 00:50:28.390
the balance between like setting the hard boundary

1194
00:50:28.390 --> 00:50:32.670
and what happens when that expectation isn't met,

1195
00:50:32.670 --> 00:50:36.280
how hard do you come down on that kid because,

1196
00:50:36.280 --> 00:50:39.080
especially when they're in those teenage years,

1197
00:50:39.080 --> 00:50:41.420
like the communication is so critical

1198
00:50:41.420 --> 00:50:44.900
and I don't wanna do anything that's gonna shut that down

1199
00:50:44.900 --> 00:50:47.160
and if I come in really hot,

1200
00:50:47.160 --> 00:50:50.593
I risk them just clamping down and then

1201
00:50:50.593 --> 00:50:53.760
they're not telling me what's going on in their life anymore

1202
00:50:53.760 --> 00:50:56.440
and keeping that communication open, I think is critical,

1203
00:50:56.440 --> 00:50:58.340
when they're trying to figure out

1204
00:50:58.340 --> 00:50:59.680
how to navigate

1205
00:51:00.520 --> 00:51:02.640
their social circles and drugs and alcohol

1206
00:51:02.640 --> 00:51:05.030
and the temptations that teenagers face,

1207
00:51:05.030 --> 00:51:07.320
like, I want them to feel like they can come and talk to me

1208
00:51:07.320 --> 00:51:10.550
about that stuff, but if I'm too disciplinarian,

1209
00:51:10.550 --> 00:51:12.030
then they're gonna,

1210
00:51:12.030 --> 00:51:14.600
I'm not gonna be the guy that they're gonna come to

1211
00:51:14.600 --> 00:51:17.960
when they need to like, clear that stuff.

1212
00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:19.780
Well, in our family

1213
00:51:19.780 --> 00:51:23.120
and I didn't go in with this plan, it just happened,

1214
00:51:23.120 --> 00:51:25.250
so, it's a good model to use,

1215
00:51:25.250 --> 00:51:27.580
my wife is the pressure relief valve

1216
00:51:27.580 --> 00:51:29.390
so, they'll go to my wife

1217
00:51:29.390 --> 00:51:32.120
and they'll open up and talk to her about anything.

1218
00:51:32.120 --> 00:51:33.110
There's no doubt about it

1219
00:51:33.110 --> 00:51:36.220
that based on my communication and our family,

1220
00:51:36.220 --> 00:51:40.200
they know that I'm hardcore and I have high expectations,

1221
00:51:40.200 --> 00:51:43.366
I've tried really hard to explain that my expectations,

1222
00:51:43.366 --> 00:51:46.035
I don't care if you get A pluses, I really,

1223
00:51:46.035 --> 00:51:49.314
by the way, they're all striving for A,

1224
00:51:49.314 --> 00:51:50.599
I didn't get A's, I gotta C's at school.

1225
00:51:50.599 --> 00:51:52.280
They're all like speak Mandarin and stuff.

1226
00:51:52.280 --> 00:51:55.680
They all speak Mandarin, they do extra math but

1227
00:51:56.800 --> 00:51:58.560
I know some people are rolling their eyes

1228
00:51:58.560 --> 00:52:00.660
that might be listening, it's like

1229
00:52:01.730 --> 00:52:04.300
again, we don't take tests in Mandarin

1230
00:52:04.300 --> 00:52:06.170
where my expectation, you've gotta do this or that, no,

1231
00:52:06.170 --> 00:52:08.010
you just gotta, it's kind of like

1232
00:52:08.010 --> 00:52:09.740
you gotta exercise a little bit each day,

1233
00:52:09.740 --> 00:52:12.690
you gotta speak another language with a teacher every day.

1234
00:52:12.690 --> 00:52:14.220
Well, the Mandarin thing started because

1235
00:52:14.220 --> 00:52:16.360
your daughter came to you and said,

1236
00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:18.280
I wanna learn Mandarin, right?

1237
00:52:18.280 --> 00:52:21.090
It was a little bit of that, it was,

1238
00:52:21.090 --> 00:52:22.858
we had a Kung Fu master living

1239
00:52:22.858 --> 00:52:25.117
(laughs) in our house which was,

1240
00:52:26.460 --> 00:52:29.240
it was another story.
How old were they then?

1241
00:52:29.240 --> 00:52:30.610
They were very young,

1242
00:52:30.610 --> 00:52:33.140
I saw the movie "Kill Bill" when they were young

1243
00:52:33.140 --> 00:52:36.480
and Uma Thurman had a Kung Fu master

1244
00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:37.817
and I turned to my wife and I said,

1245
00:52:37.817 --> 00:52:40.090
"Wouldn't it be unbelievable

1246
00:52:40.090 --> 00:52:43.260
if we had a Kung Fu master living on the farm?"

1247
00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:45.660
So, I called a friend of a friend of a friend

1248
00:52:45.660 --> 00:52:48.550
and I convinced a guy to come from China,

1249
00:52:48.550 --> 00:52:50.730
who didn't speak any English

1250
00:52:50.730 --> 00:52:53.950
and would train them every day in Kung Fu,

1251
00:52:53.950 --> 00:52:56.220
and then my daughter said, "Hey, I'd love to learn

1252
00:52:56.220 --> 00:52:57.727
so then it started, so."

1253
00:52:57.727 --> 00:53:00.010
Did you take the Kung Fu also?

1254
00:53:00.010 --> 00:53:01.130
I didn't.
You didn't.

1255
00:53:01.130 --> 00:53:03.700
I didn't, I'm a wimp.

1256
00:53:03.700 --> 00:53:05.500
What did they learn from that?

1257
00:53:05.500 --> 00:53:06.630
Discipline,

1258
00:53:06.630 --> 00:53:08.680
tremendous discipline and it's really,

1259
00:53:08.680 --> 00:53:12.580
the Kung Fu is kinda like almost gymnastics, if you will,

1260
00:53:12.580 --> 00:53:16.530
so, they had gymnastics class with a Mandarin-speaking

1261
00:53:16.530 --> 00:53:18.980
master for three or four years,

1262
00:53:18.980 --> 00:53:21.423
before we switched to traditional sports.

1263
00:53:22.850 --> 00:53:26.470
Yeah, I think from all of this,

1264
00:53:26.470 --> 00:53:28.390
if you could drill down on the message,

1265
00:53:28.390 --> 00:53:32.340
it's really like, get in the habit of doing hard things,

1266
00:53:32.340 --> 00:53:35.570
learn how to face your fears and walk through them,

1267
00:53:35.570 --> 00:53:37.380
don't be afraid when you fall down,

1268
00:53:37.380 --> 00:53:39.540
like failing is part of this whole thing,

1269
00:53:39.540 --> 00:53:42.336
get used to it, acclimate to it

1270
00:53:42.336 --> 00:53:45.490
and just develop that skill set

1271
00:53:45.490 --> 00:53:48.850
where you're used to facing obstacles

1272
00:53:48.850 --> 00:53:51.340
and weathering through them because life is hard

1273
00:53:51.340 --> 00:53:54.190
and you're gonna have to deal with that at some point

1274
00:53:54.190 --> 00:53:56.390
